#N canvas 20 20 2760 2120 12;
#X text 20 20 u_tempo -- the MASTER REFERENCE. It owns the master BPM \, the pulse oscillator that MIDI clock is cut from \, and the transport. It is NOT the clock: Cut It runs poly-tempo and every part that needs a beat owns a c_clock instance of its own. See ref/module/tempo.md., f 58;
#X text 20 140 creation args: none. inlets: none. outlets: none. It talks on tempo \, clock \, start \, stop \, panic \, disp and err \, and out of THREE MIDI ports -- two carry the clock and the transport \, and a third takes panic's STOP alone. See realtime-out., f 58;
#X obj 20 300 r tempo;
#X obj 20 345 t f f;
#X obj 20 400 clip 5 600;
#X obj 20 445 t f f f;
#X obj 450 470 s \$0-clamped;
#X obj 250 505 s \$0-bpm;
#X obj 20 545 / 60;
#X obj 20 590 * 24;
#X obj 20 645 phasor~;
#X obj 20 705 threshold~ 0.5 0 0.1 0;
#X obj 20 770 t b b;
#X obj 20 830 f;
#X obj 20 875 + 1;
#X obj 20 920 mod 24;
#X obj 140 965 t f f;
#X obj 140 1030 select 0;
#X obj 140 1075 s clock;
#X obj 200 555 r \$0-reset;
#X msg 200 600 0;
#X obj 330 615 r \$0-reset;
#X msg 330 660 0;
#X obj 480 715 r \$0-reset;
#X msg 480 760 23;
#X msg 300 830 248;
#X obj 300 875 s \$0-rt;
#N canvas 40 40 1200 840 range-check 0;
#X text 20 20 The raw value arrives AFTER the clamped one -- it comes off the left outlet of a trigger whose right outlet ran the whole clamp chain first. So the comparison always has something true to compare against \, with no delay object anywhere., f 58;
#X obj 20 170 inlet;
#X obj 20 270 !=;
#X obj 240 170 r \$0-clamped;
#X obj 20 220 change;
#X obj 20 320 select 1;
#X msg 20 370 warn u_tempo bpm-out-of-range;
#X obj 20 420 s err;
#X text 20 490 THE change IS ON THE VALUE \, NOT ON THE VERDICT \, and getting that backwards was a real bug. Filtering the out-of-range FLAG means 500 warns and a 5 sent straight after it does not -- the flag never changed \, so a second and quite different fault is silent. Filtering the VALUE keeps the only case worth suppressing: the same number arriving over and over from a control parked past the end., f 58;
#X text 20 660 select 1's reject carries the 0 it did not match and goes nowhere \, which is the safe way to treat any reject. u_err's alert text is one symbol of 21 characters or fewer \; bpm-out-of-range is 16., f 58;
#X connect 1 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 0 2 0;
#X connect 3 0 2 1;
#X connect 2 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X restore 700 400 pd range-check;
#N canvas 40 40 1300 820 realtime-out 0;
#X text 20 20 The CLOCK and the transport leave here \, on TWO ports. Port 1 is the Launchpad \, which paces its own flash and pulse animations from incoming beat clock \; port 3 is the SP-404 \, whose BPM SYNC time-stretch learns tempo ONLY by measuring pulse intervals. wire.sh already wires Pure Data:4 and Pure Data:6. A THIRD PORT TAKES THE PANIC STOP ALONE -- see the right., f 58;
#X obj 20 200 r \$0-rt;
#X obj 20 250 t f f;
#X obj 20 320 midiout;
#X obj 300 320 midiout;
#X obj 700 150 loadbang;
#X obj 700 195 t b b b;
#X msg 700 250 1;
#X msg 860 250 3;
#X text 20 420 THE PORT IS SET INTO THE COLD INLET AT LOAD \, which is u_init's proven pattern for the Launchpad SysEx. Pd 0.49 does not warn about extra creation arguments -- [loadbang 7] loads silently -- so a creation argument could not be CONFIRMED to reach anything \, only assumed to. This way there is nothing to assume., f 58;
#X text 20 570 A nonexistent port is silently dropped \, so on the Mac -- where port 3 usually does not exist -- this costs nothing and prints nothing. There is no port-unavailable string in the 0.49 binary at all., f 58;
#X text 20 690 The trigger is not decoration: two destinations is a fan-out \, and undefined order between two MIDI ports is exactly the kind of thing that would only show up as a device drifting. The port-4 box below is fed from its own receive \, so it is not part of that fan-out at all., f 58;
#X obj 580 200 r \$0-rt4;
#X obj 580 320 midiout;
#X msg 1020 250 4;
#X text 580 420 ⛔ PORT 4 GETS THE PANIC STOP AND NOT THE CLOCK \, and the separate receive is what makes that true. \$0-rt4 is written in exactly one place -- the panic branch on the parent canvas -- so no clock byte can reach it however this file is edited later. Item 279 measured ports 2 and 4 getting NOTHING at all \, which is a bug for the Volca: it is a synthesiser with a sequencer \, and one still running through a panic is the case panic exists for. ⚠️ WIDENING THE CLOCK TO ALL FOUR IS A SEPARATE QUESTION and is parked in plan-v04 -- the Launchpad ignores incoming clock in Programmer Mode \, so ONE of the two ports fed above is already pointless \, and which devices Cut It intends to DRIVE has to be decided before adding more. The Volca honours Start/Stop only when its own MIDI Clock src is set to Auto -- that is Korg's documentation and has not been measured here \, so it is a bench step rather than an assumption. See ref/device/volca.md., f 44;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 0 4 0;
#X connect 2 1 3 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 6 1 8 0;
#X connect 7 0 3 1;
#X connect 8 0 4 1;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
#X connect 6 2 14 0;
#X connect 14 0 13 1;
#X restore 300 940 pd realtime-out;
#X obj 900 300 r start;
#X obj 900 345 t b b b b;
#X obj 1150 410 s \$0-reset;
#X msg 1040 410 250;
#X obj 1040 455 s \$0-rt;
#X msg 900 410 led running;
#X obj 900 455 s disp;
#X obj 900 530 r stop;
#X obj 900 575 t b b b;
#X msg 1040 640 252;
#X obj 1040 685 s \$0-rt;
#X msg 900 640 led stopped;
#X obj 900 685 s disp;
#X obj 900 760 r panic;
#X obj 900 805 t b b b;
#X msg 1220 870 252;
#X obj 1220 915 s \$0-rt;
#X text 20 1700 ALL NOTES OFF MOVED TO m_404 IN v0.3 \, and the two boxes that did it stood here. It sent CC 123 on channel 33 alone -- bank A \, ONE TENTH of the instrument -- because it was written before any file owned the 404. The device's owner owns its panic \, the same argument m_launchpad makes for owning its own safe exit \, and m_404 now sends it on all ten channels., f 80;
#X text 1200 1700 ⚠️ u_tempo STILL OWNS THE 252 \, which is the realtime STOP. What left is only the note-silencing \, which is per-device and per-channel and was never this file's to know about. ⛔ THIS COMMENT SAID reaches every port UNTIL ITS PORTS WERE COUNTED \, and it was wrong: realtime-out held two midiout boxes \, on ports 1 and 3 \, so the Volca and the nano got nothing. Item 279. The panic branch now sends the 252 to port 4 as well \, through its own receive -- see realtime-out. The CLOCK is still two ports and that is deliberate., f 80;
#X msg 900 870 led panic;
#X obj 900 915 s disp;
#X obj 1450 300 r \$0-bpm;
#X obj 1450 345 t b f;
#X obj 1450 410 f;
#X obj 1450 455 makefilename %g-bpm;
#X obj 1450 500 list prepend status;
#X obj 1450 545 list trim;
#X obj 1450 590 s disp;
#X obj 1750 180 loadbang;
#X obj 1750 225 t b b;
#X obj 1750 270 del 4000;
#X obj 1900 270 del 200;
#X obj 1900 315 t b b;
#X msg 1900 420 120;
#X obj 1900 465 s tempo;
#X msg 2050 365 led stopped;
#X obj 2050 410 s disp;
#X text 20 1160 WHAT AUDIO-DOMAIN BUYS \, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It does NOT reduce the jitter on the outgoing MIDI byte: threshold~ reports on a 64-sample block boundary exactly as metro fires on one \, so the 1.45 ms is unchanged and is not fixable in vanilla Pd. What it buys is a rate change that is phase-continuous and glitch-free \, and ONE phase that grain-rate code can read as a signal -- so MIDI clock and grain timing cannot drift apart. Written down here because otherwise somebody will try to fix the jitter., f 58;
#X text 20 1360 THE COUNTER RESETS TO 23 \, NOT 0. The next pulse adds one and lands on 0 \, which is the beat. Reset it to 0 and the first beat after every start is silently skipped -- 24 pulses late \, once \, which is exactly the kind of thing nobody notices until a pattern is a beat out., f 58;
#X text 780 1160 CLOCK NEVER STOPS. 248 flows whether the transport is running or not \; only 250 and 252 mark transport. Stop the stream and the 404 stretches its samples to whatever tempo it last measured \, so a stopped clock is a wrong tempo rather than no tempo. This is the least obvious thing in the file., f 44;
#X text 780 1330 NEVER 251. Continue means resume from a stored position and nothing in this rig has Song Position Pointer \, so sending it would be a lie that some future device might believe., f 44;
#X text 780 1460 panic sends 252 -- the realtime STOP -- to ports 1 \, 3 and 4 \, and lights the aux LED \, and leaves the clock running. ⛔ PORT 4 IS SENT SEPARATELY AND ONLY HERE. The clock reaches two ports \; the STOP reaches three \, because a Volca that is sequencing through a panic is the case this exists for. Item 279 \, and realtime-out says why the two are not the same question. SILENCING NOTES IS NO LONGER DONE HERE: each device layer owns its own \, because the channels are the device's business. m_404 sends All Notes Off across all ten of its banks and m_volca sends CC 123 -- its makenote no longer covers everything \, since volca-key bypasses it., f 44;
#X text 1450 700 THE FOOTER \, NOT THE PARAM LAYER. status is sticky and always visible \, which is right for a tempo \, and it supersedes u_init's v0.3-ready once the clock is up. panic still overwrites it \, correctly. The f holds the value so the footer can be redrawn without a new tempo arriving., f 44;
#X text 1450 870 TWO LOADBANGS \, TWO REASONS. del 200 publishes 120 on tempo ONCE -- the only time this file writes that bus -- so the phasor starts and anything that came up first still gets a value. del 4000 redraws the footer only \, because u_init's boot sequence puts v0.3-ready there at about 3.5 s. THAT NUMBER IS COUPLED TO u_init'S TIMINGS and u_init says so too., f 44;
#X text 1450 1070 It stores what it hears and never re-emits \, so there is no loop between this file and the bus. Anything may write tempo \; only this file owns what happens next., f 44;
#X text 300 1000 THREE RECEIVES \, ONE NAME \, AND THE ORDER DOES NOT MATTER -- which is why they are receives and not a trigger. All three write COLD inlets and none of them fires anything: the phase \, the threshold~ state and the counter are simply put back where a start wants them \, and the next block does the rest., f 44;
#X text 620 1000 threshold~ takes a 0 in its right inlet to force the state low \, with no debounce -- straight out of its help patch. Without it a start arriving while the phasor is above 0.5 would wait for a wrap before the first pulse., f 30;
#X text 1450 1250 BOTH DEBOUNCES ARE ZERO \, AND THAT IS A MEASURED DECISION RATHER THAN A TIDY DEFAULT. threshold~ decrements its dead time once per DSP BLOCK \, not per millisecond \, so any non-zero debounce burns a whole block on every state change and the clock silently loses pulses at speed. A phasor cannot bounce \, so there was never anything for a debounce to protect against. The ceiling this buys \, and how much headroom the clamp of 600 leaves \, are item 58 on ref/module/tempo.md., f 44;
#X obj 1330 410 s \$0-refresh;
#X obj 1330 640 s \$0-refresh;
#X obj 1620 420 r \$0-refresh;
#X text 1900 1250 THE FOOTER IS REDRAWN ON EVERY TRANSPORT CHANGE \, and that is not cosmetic. status is STICKY \, so panic sat in the footer until the next tempo message -- you could start the transport \, watch the LED go green and the 404 start \, and still be told panic. Found on the device. A start or a stop now bangs the stored BPM back out \, so the footer always describes the state you are actually in., f 44;
#X obj 1900 370 spigot 1;
#X obj 2280 225 r tempo;
#X obj 2280 270 t b;
#X msg 2280 315 0;
#X text 2280 380 THE 120 IS A FALLBACK \, NOT A DEFAULT \, and this spigot is what makes the difference. mother pushes the real knob positions at load \, so knob 1 usually sets the tempo before this fires -- but WHICH ARRIVES FIRST IS A RACE \, and it was seen going both ways on the same device: booting at 10 BPM one day and 120 the next. Any tempo at all closes this gate \, so the seed can only ever fill a silence. It still matters on the Mac \, where nothing pushes a knob position at all., f 50;
#X msg 1220 990 252;
#X obj 1220 1035 s \$0-rt4;
#X text 620 1810 ⛔ THE SAME 252 \, SENT AGAIN \, TO A RECEIVE NOTHING ELSE WRITES. It is deliberately not a fan-out off the message box beside it and deliberately not a third outlet on realtime-out's trigger: this byte has to reach port 4 without the CLOCK reaching it too \, and a shared path could not promise that. realtime-out says the rest. ⚠️ IT FIRES FIRST \, off the panic trigger's rightmost outlet \, because the Volca is the longest path in the rig -- USB to a DIN interface to a wire., f 44;
